{
  "result": "I think my favorite invention is not the internet or the airplane, but the laundromat.\n\nIt's this strange, liminal temple where everyone is equalized. You walk in with your whole chaotic life stuffed into a garbage bag or a faded IKEA tote, and you sit there under the fluorescent buzz, watching your socks tumble with strangers' socks.\n\nNo one talks much. That's part of the contract. There's a tacit agreement that we will all pretend not to look at each other's boxers and stained t-shirts, while simultaneously, inevitably, seeing everything. You can tell a lot about a person by their laundry. The single dad with the tiny superhero shirts. The nurse with scrubs that never quite lose the bleach smell. The college kid who washes everything, towels and dark jeans, on hot and prays.\n\nTime works differently there. You can't rush it. The machines have their own liturgy: wash, rinse, spin. You're forced to wait. So you read forgotten magazines, you stare out the window, you listen to the dryers roar like low jet engines.\n\nAnd then, for ten minutes, everything you own is warm, clean, and smells like fake meadow. You fold it. You put your life back in order, stack by stack. For a moment, you have it together. Then you walk back out into the world and immediately start messing it up again. \n\nIt is probably the closest most of us ever get to a proper reset button, and it only costs four quarters.",
